Fillets: one ball rolling over a narrow face between two stripes makes one
blend that face cuts (a notch in the blend's side); stripes under such a
ball are dropped; a blend a face of the solid pokes through gets a hole,
that face keeping its part outside the blend (mitcad#343).

The case: a big torus's edges with a plane rounded with 0.5, the chain
broken by two narrow strips of a boss (a cylinder standing on the plane)
between the torus and earlier roundings of 0.6 tangent to the plane, onto
which the plane's contact runs. The ball keeps touching the torus and the
earlier rounding over each strip; the strip's edge with the torus lies
under it, and the boss cuts the blend: over one strip from where the
contact on the earlier rounding leaves it across the strip's edge to where
it comes back, the cut reaching back along the blend past both points,
over the other in a closed curve inside the blend. Of each strip a lens is
left. Before, each stripe ended at its vertex of the strip with a corner,
which failed ("Failed to find edge").

- ChFi3d_Builder::JoinOverNarrowFaces (new file ChFi3d_Builder_Trim.cxx),
  called in Compute after RemoveCoincidentStripes: two stripe ends of one
  radius on the same faces Fo and N, the contact on N of one ending on an
  edge of a narrow face C holding both ends' vertices, no other stripe at
  them but stripes whose edges lie between Fo and C. The first end's blend
  is walked again from a section some radii before its end, N's surface
  unbounded, along a guide through the ball's centres of both ends' pieces
  (A's own guide runs straight on past its spine and its sections slide
  along the ball's path). Its contact leaves N across an edge of C and
  comes back; C's cut of the walked blend between those points (the lines
  of GeomInt_IntSS joined end to end) may reach back past both. The walk
  is three pieces on one surface (one face in ChFi3d_FilDS), the middle
  one's side on C the cut. The other end's piece is cut back to the section
  of the same ball beyond the cut, where the two ends meet as free ends
  with a common section edge; A's end piece is cut back to the walk's
  start. Analytic ends are walked the same way.
- Stripes whose edges all lie between Fo and a narrow face C under another
  stripe's blend on Fo and a face other than C (its contact on Fo running
  along them within a piece, on the side its blend covers) are dropped,
  unless the ball touches C (a wall beyond a rib, on from the other face).
- ChFi3d_Builder::PierceBlends, after ChFi3d_FilDS of each stripe: where
  an edge of Fo with a face C lies under the blend, C's surface cuts a
  piece's surface in a closed curve inside it, and every edge of C lies
  under the blend, the curve is a hole of the blend's face and the edge of
  C's part outside the blend; C's edges are cut at their vertices.

